cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "parmar: a subword-tokenization pre-filter for byte-level entropy coders, and its stress-test harness"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Parmar
    given-names: Ronak
    email: ronakparmar2428@gmail.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/shallowbyte/parmar"
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  parmar replaces UTF-8 text with BPE token IDs before handing it to a byte-level
  entropy coder (LZMA2, zstd, gzip, bzip2), on the premise that the ~45% denser
  token stream lets a fixed-size dictionary window span proportionally more original
  text. The accompanying harness tests that claim across a 64MB-4GB corpus sweep of
  PG-19, with mandatory sha256 round-trip verification on every measured
  configuration. Measured result: the ratio gap over raw bytes widens with corpus
  size for large-window backends and plateaus once the corpus is well past that
  window, while remaining flat for gzip's 32 KiB window - identifying window
  expansion and representation density as two separable effects.
keywords:
  - compression
  - tokenization
  - byte-pair-encoding
  - lzma
  - zstd
  - benchmark

references:
  - type: article
    title: "Compressive Transformers for Long-Range Sequence Modelling"
    authors:
      - family-names: Rae
        given-names: "Jack W."
      - family-names: Potapenko
        given-names: Anna
      - family-names: Jayakumar
        given-names: "Siddhant M."
      - family-names: Hillier
        given-names: Chloe
      - family-names: Lillicrap
        given-names: "Timothy P."
    year: 2019
    url: "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.05507"
    notes: "Source of the PG-19 corpus used for every measurement in this repository."
